edb/ref 1077 "field-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487282 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186486643 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487282 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fadb4645-7f64-5b96-9f43-b05796106137" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487294 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487142 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487294 #edb/ref 1069 "q-253513" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487294 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487294 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following set.\n\n$$\nA=\\{3-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}\\,:\\,n\\in N\\}\n$$\n\nFor this set, we have that {{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487294 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "13b42a92-bc61-5733-b661-85ce69e84b97" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487294 #edb/ref 1074 "The infimum of $A$ is the greatest lower bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=3-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/35/351e7bb3db6811be2a690fbd3fc2dc99282c4ff676e9116717d85515a298cf80.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is increasing and converges to $3.$ The minimum of $A$ is \n\n$$\nmin(A)={a}_{1}=3-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{1}=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $x\\le {a}_{1}$ is a lower bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $x>{a}_{1}$ cannot be a lower bound because ${a}_{1},$ an element of $A,$ is smaller than $x.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{2}]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\ninf(A)=max(-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{2}]=\\frac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\frac{5}{2}$ is the minimum of $A.$ Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ninf(A)=\\frac{5}{2}\\in A.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487298 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487173 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487298 #edb/ref 1069 "q-253896" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487298 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487298 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/0d/0d745d274250c84f6701ff22d17d0c6506a9f03f4a1a461e09f609a0ea11d99b.png)\n\nWhat is the supremum of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above?\n\n${sup}_{x\\in R}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487298 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1a350bae-823f-50ee-90ca-2add36d5d3d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487298 #edb/ref 1074 "The supremum of a function is the supremum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=[-2,1).$\n\nTo find the supremum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $y\\ge 1$ is an upper bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $y<1$ is **not** an upper bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $A$ that is greater than $y$ for any $y<1.$\n\nTherefore, $[1,\\infty )$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\n{sup}_{x\\in R}f(x)=sup(A)=min[1,\\infty )=1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487328 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487255 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487328 #edb/ref 1069 "q-253541" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487328 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487328 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the infimum of the set $B=\\{-0.15,\\,-0.2,\\,0.05,\\,0,\\,9\\}?$\n\n$inf(B)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487328 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "546541ff-37d6-5bb0-b37e-29f5b9d47681" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487328 #edb/ref 1074 "The infimum of a set $B$ is the greatest lower bound of $B.$\n\nIf $B$ is a finite set, the infimum of $B$ equals its minimum. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}inf(B) & =min\\{-0.15,\\,-0.2,\\,0.05,\\,0,\\,9\\}=-0.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487329 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487251 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487329 #edb/ref 1069 "q-252987" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487329 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487329 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the supremum of the set $A=\\{-1,\\,0,\\,1,\\,2\\}?$\n\n$sup(A)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487329 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "56e0e5d9-1f02-588c-913a-41be6c70db4a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487329 #edb/ref 1074 "The supremum of a set $A$ is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nIf $A$ is a finite set, the supremum of $A$ equals its maximum. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sup(A) & =max\\{-1,\\,0,\\,1,\\,2\\}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487332 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487332 #edb/ref 1069 "q-253899" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487332 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487332 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/56/567aa249f2ed84b537d20d4137a28e0df1cd3e4c942f4cf305bd864da32b8bac.png)\n\nWhat is the supremum of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above?\n\n$\\,{sup}_{x\\ge -1}f(x)$\n {{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487332 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5b171f8f-d26a-5943-99d9-8e4c4110d0e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487332 #edb/ref 1074 "The supremum of a function is the supremum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=[0,\\infty ).$\n\nTo find the supremum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nIn our case, $A$ has no upper bounds. We can always find an element $x$ such that $f(x)$ is greater than $y$ for any $y.$ \n\nTherefore, ${sup}_{x\\ge -1}f(x)$ $=+\\infty .$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487336 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487155 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487336 #edb/ref 1069 "q-253550" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487336 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487336 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the set $A,$ defined as\n\n$$\nA=\\{\\frac{1}{5-n}:n\\in \\{0,1,2,3,4\\}\\}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the supremum of $A?$\n\n$sup(A)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487336 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5d279c4a-988e-5c29-a6a0-5c2bb436ded0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487336 #edb/ref 1074 "The supremum of a set $A$ is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nIf $A$ is a finite set, the supremum of $A$ equals its maximum. \n\nFirst, let's write down all the terms of our set by substituting $n=1,2,3,4$ into the expression for the elements of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5-0} & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\frac{1}{5-1} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{5-2} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{5-3} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{5-4} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\{\\frac{1}{5},\\,\\frac{1}{4},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{2},\\,1\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sup(A) & =max\\{\\frac{1}{5},\\,\\frac{1}{4},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{2},\\,1\\}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487338 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487338 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487338 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487227 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487338 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487264 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487338 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487265 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487338 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487275 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487338 #edb/ref 1069 "q-253560" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487338 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487338 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the set $B=(-1,\\infty ).$ Complete the following reasoning to determine $inf(B)$ and $sup(B).$\n\nThe infimum of $B$ is the {{field-1}} of $B,$ and the supremum is the {{field-2}} of $B.$\n\nSince $B$ is {{field-3}}, we have $inf(B)$ {{field-4}}.\n\nSince $B$ is {{field-5}}, we have $sup(B)$ {{field-6}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487338 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "60981b2b-035b-5158-a36c-3b0a5f268ae1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487338 #edb/ref 1074 "The infimum of $B$ is the greatest lower bound of $B,$ and the supremum is the smallest upper bound of $B.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $B$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- Any number in the set $x\\le -1$ is smaller than or equal to the elements of $B.$ Thus, the set $(-\\infty ,-1]$ contains only lower bounds of $B.$\n\n- Any number in the set $x>-1$ is **not** a lower bound of $B.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $B$ that is smaller than $x$ for any $x>-1.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,-1]$ is the set of lower bounds of $B,$ and\n\n$$\ninf(B)=max(-\\infty ,-1]=-1.\n$$\n\nNext, to find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $B$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nSince $B$ is unbounded above, $B$ has no upper bounds, and $sup(B)=+\\infty .$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe infimum of a set $B$ is the $\\text{greatest lower bound}$ of $B,$ and the supremum is the $\\text{smallest upper bound}$ of $B.$\n\nSince $B$ is $\\text{bounded below},$ we have $inf(B)=-1.$\n\nSince $B$ is $\\text{unbounded above},$ we have $sup(B)=+\\infty .$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487344 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487258 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487344 #edb/ref 1069 "q-253831" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487344 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487344 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following set.\n\n$$\nA=\\{2+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}\\,:\\,n\\in N\\}\n$$\n\nFor this set, we have that {{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487344 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "79e51d03-1539-5ef1-be77-7756304d3608" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487344 #edb/ref 1074 "The infimum of $A$ is the greatest lower bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=2+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/78/7879f99e6dd09e7ada4c1d920b78504df70bf0c511380ea21728a755d6590d23.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is decreasing and converges to $2.$ \n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number in the set $x\\le 2$ is a lower bound of $A,$\n\n- any number in the set $x>2$ is **not** a lower bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find a sufficiently large $n$ so that $2+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}$ is smaller than $x$ for any $x>2.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,2]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\ninf(A)=max(-\\infty ,2]=2.\n$$\n\nNotice that $2$ is *not* the minimum of $A$ (the set $A$ has no minimum). Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ninf(A)=2\\notin A.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487350 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487266 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487350 #edb/ref 1069 "q-253859" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487350 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487350 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/37/372aa80e15465694347dce5325537a10be1217e83050d609dfeec0347bad1116.png)\n\nWhat is the infimum of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above?\n\n${inf}_{x\\in R}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487350 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "956f7419-f174-5327-980b-e8eb653a771d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487350 #edb/ref 1074 "The infimum of a function is the infimum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=[-2,2].$\n\nTo find the infimum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $y\\le -2$ is a lower bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $y>-2$ is **not** a lower bound of $A$ because $-2,$ an element of $A,$ is smaller than $y.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,-2]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\n{inf}_{x\\in R}f(x)=inf(A)=max(-\\infty ,-2]=-2.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487359 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487220 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487359 #edb/ref 1069 "q-252989" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487359 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487359 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the set $A,$ defined as\n\n$$\nA=\\{\\frac{1}{n+2}:n\\in \\{1,2,3,4\\}\\}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the infimum of $A?$\n\n$inf(A)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487359 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a2831798-1168-5ea1-b772-79e02ad4074c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487359 #edb/ref 1074 "The infimum of a set $A$ is the greatest lower bound of $A.$\n\nIf $A$ is a finite set, the infimum of $A$ equals its minimum.\n\nFirst, let's write down all the terms of our set by substituting $n=1,2,3,4$ into the expression for the elements of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(1)+2} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{(2)+2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{(3)+2} & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\frac{1}{(4)+2} & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\{\\frac{1}{6},\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{3}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}inf(A) & =min\\{\\frac{1}{6},\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{3}\\}=\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487363 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487363 #edb/ref 1069 "q-253525" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487363 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487363 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following set.\n\n$$\nA=\\{2-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{n}\\,:\\,n\\in N\\}\n$$\n\nFor this set, we have that {{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487363 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a4eaf9cf-b8c1-5f93-bf45-1dc2cc8b5b67" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487363 #edb/ref 1074 "The infimum of $A$ is the greatest lower bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=2-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{n},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/72/72fa47f25042b5187a87262b23e0976429909011b1516bc6018e70cd4f881f11.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is increasing and converges to $5.$ The minimum of $A$ is \n\n$$\nmin(A)={a}_{1}=2-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{1}=\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $x\\le {a}_{1}$ is a lower bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $x>{a}_{1}$ cannot be a lower bound because ${a}_{1},$ an element of $A,$ is smaller than $x.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{3}]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\ninf(A)=max(-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{3}]=\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\frac{5}{3}$ is the minimum of $A.$ Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ninf(A)=\\frac{5}{3}\\in A.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487366 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487257 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487366 #edb/ref 1069 "q-253910" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487366 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487366 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following set.\n\n$$\nA=\\{\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n}\\,:\\,n\\in N\\}\n$$\n\nFor this set, we have that {{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487366 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aafdf357-e5ff-5cb5-9dea-31946c44ff01" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487366 #edb/ref 1074 "The infimum of $A$ is the greatest lower bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/42/42f6ea3d1c14b0ccb8774e284dc6812486ace7eda512e9c1b94df27e09445e6e.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is an oscillating sequence converging to $0.$ The minimum of $A$ is \n\n$$\nmin(A)={a}_{2}=\\frac{(-1)^{2+1}}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $x\\le {a}_{2}$ is a lower bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $x>{a}_{2}$ cannot be a lower bound because ${a}_{2},$ an element of $A,$ is smaller than $x.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{2}]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\ninf(A)=max(-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{2}]=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $-\\frac{1}{2}$ is the minimum of $A.$ Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ninf(A)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\in A.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487375 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487172 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487375 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487375 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487252 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487375 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487375 #edb/ref 1069 "q-253556" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487375 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487375 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the set $C=(-2,-1].$ Complete the following reasoning to identify $sup(C).$\n\nThe supremum of a set $C$ is the {{field-1}} of $C.$\n\nThe set of upper bounds of $C$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x{{field-2}}{{field-3}}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, {{field-4}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487375 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "be9aa3b1-68a0-5981-aa70-f10a99f6fbac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487375 #edb/ref 1074 "The supremum of a set $C$ is the sm