$\n\n- Any number in the set $x<\\pi $ is **not** an upper bound of $B.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $B$ that is greater than $x$ for any $x<\\pi .$\n\nTherefore, $[\\pi ,\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $B,$ and\n\n$$\nsup(B)=min\\,[\\pi ,\\infty )=\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe infimum of a set $B$ is the $\\text{greatest lower bound}$ of $B,$ and the supremum is the $\\text{smallest upper bound}$ of $B.$\n\nSince $B$ is $\\text{unbounded below},$ we have $inf(B)=-\\infty .$\n\nSince $B$ is $\\text{bounded above},$ we have $sup(B)=\\pi .$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487405 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487405 #edb/ref 1069 "q-253912" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487405 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487405 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following set.\n\n$$\nA=\\{-1+{(\\frac{7}{10})}^{n}\\,:\\,n\\in N\\}\n$$\n\nFor this set, we have that {{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487405 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fd2b3866-cbeb-5f5a-bcbc-008f4c1cdcd4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487405 #edb/ref 1074 "The infimum of $A$ is the greatest lower bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=-1+{(\\frac{7}{10})}^{n},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/8b/8be6d8824c8cbf1fb66d64d3ccc027f2949149adc9f95cf4d1f3a814baec80b3.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is decreasing and converges to $-1.$ \n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number in the set $x\\le -1$ is a lower bound of $A,$\n\n- any number in the set $x>-1$ is **not** a lower bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find a sufficiently large $n$ so that $-1+{(\\frac{7}{10})}^{n}$ is smaller than $x$ for any $x>-1.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,-1]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\ninf(A)=max(-\\infty ,-1]=-1.\n$$\n\nNotice that $-1$ is *not* the minimum of $A$ (the set $A$ has no minimum). Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ninf(A)=-1\\notin A.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
